WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:09.500
PROFESSOR: Both experts and non-experts
had a role in Roman divination.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:16.470
The experts were members of priestly
groups, the haruspices and the augurs.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.060
The haurspices was
supposed to be Etruscan,

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:25.840
so for much of the history of ancient
Rome, this was a foreign specialism.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.690
The haruspices had
expertise in examining

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:34.510
the livers of animals sacrificed
before any important occasion

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.830
and in interpreting the results.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.100
One of the other specialisms
of the haruspices

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:45.740
was the interpretation of prodigies
such as eclipses, thunderbolts,

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:51.200
weird noises, and monstrous
births, like two headed animals.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.100
These were seen as signs of
disorder in the natural world.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.670
So in the case of
prodigies, we're talking

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:03.230
about the interpretation of
things that occur spontaneously,

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:08.770
not things that are done on purpose
to solicit a divine response.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:14.230
The Roman Senate, made up of current
and former elected officials,

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.380
would consult the
haruspices who would give

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:22.600
their interpretation of the prodigies
identifying which of the gods

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:28.180
had been offended or what religious
offenses had been committed.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.710
The Senate would then decide
what to do to put things right.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:36.610
This combination of
particular religious expertise

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:42.540
and a more general level of experience
is typical of Roman prediction methods.

